window.BIO_MODEL_ANSWERS = {

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.1": "The three structural features of all virus particles are: genetic material (DNA or RNA), a capsid (protein coat), and attachment proteins (glycoproteins). The capsid protects the genetic material from enzymatic digestion and the environment.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.2": "Viruses are described as acellular because they are not made of cells — they have no cell-surface membrane, no cytoplasm, and no organelles. They are non-living because they have no metabolism; they cannot independently respire, excrete, or replicate without a host cell.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.3": "Antibiotics are not effective against viruses because viruses do not carry out metabolic processes (such as cell wall synthesis or protein synthesis on 70S ribosomes) that antibiotics can disrupt.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.1": "Both chitin and cellulose have alternate monomers rotated/flipped by 180°, both are joined by glycosidic bonds, and both form straight, unbranched chains.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.2": "The tracheole walls are very thin (one cell thick), providing a short diffusion pathway for rapid gas exchange. The tracheoles branch extensively and penetrate directly into muscle fibres, reducing the diffusion distance further and providing a large surface area for diffusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.3": "The xylem being kept open as a continuous tube allows an unbroken column of water from roots to leaves with no barrier to water movement. Cohesion between water molecules (due to hydrogen bonding) maintains the water column, and the tension created by transpiration at the leaf surface pulls water upwards through the continuous tube.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.1": "Any membrane-bound organelle, for example a mitochondrion, rough endoplasmic reticulum, nucleus (nuclear envelope), or vesicle/lysosome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.2": "The Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins (forming glycoproteins) and lipids (forming glycolipids). It also produces vesicles and lysosomes, transporting substances to the cell surface membrane or for secretion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.3": "India, Europe, East Asia, Central America, South America.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.4": "229 million × 6% (cases outside Africa) × 61% = 8.38 million cases worldwide caused by P. vivax each year.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.5": "A mutation originally produced the allele for resistance to P. vivax malaria. Individuals carrying this allele were less likely to die from malaria and therefore more likely to survive, reproduce, and pass the allele to their offspring. Over many generations the frequency of the resistance allele increased in the African population through natural selection, because non-resistant individuals were more likely to die before reproducing.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.1": "The loss of microvilli reduces the surface area of the ileum lining, meaning there are fewer carrier/channel proteins for co-transport and active transport of digestion products. This reduces the absorption of glucose and amino acids. Because fewer products of digestion are absorbed into the blood, the water potential of the lumen does not decrease as much, so less water moves out of the lumen into the blood by osmosis — resulting in diarrhoea.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.2": "The anti-toxin antibody would bind to and neutralise the C. difficile toxin (causing agglutination or preventing it from binding to ileum cells), so the toxin cannot damage the intestinal lining. From Figure 4, patients with diarrhoea have the lowest anti-toxin antibody concentrations, so the passive immunity should be offered to patients who develop C. difficile infection and have low antibody concentrations (i.e. those who already have diarrhoea or are at risk).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.3": "If taken orally, the antibody (a protein) would be digested in the gut. Endopeptidases would hydrolyse the internal peptide bonds, breaking the polypeptide into shorter fragments. Exopeptidases would then remove amino acids from the terminal ends, and membrane-bound dipeptidases would hydrolyse dipeptides into individual amino acids — destroying the antibody's tertiary structure and function.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.1": "Aseptic techniques include: washing hands with soap and disinfecting surfaces; using a sterile pipette/syringe to transfer the bacterial culture; flaming the neck of the bottle after removing the lid; lifting the lid of the agar plate at an angle (keeping the lid over the plate); working close to an upward-moving Bunsen flame; using a sterile spreader to spread the culture; and placing the pipette/spreader in disinfectant immediately after use.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.2": "Volume = πr²l → l = V ÷ (πr²) = 50 ÷ (3.14 × 3²) = 50 ÷ 28.26 = 1.77 mm.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.3": "Positive control: a known antimicrobial/antibiotic solution (added to the well to produce a clear zone, confirming the method can detect antimicrobial activity).\nNegative control: sterile water, or oil without cinnamon (added to the well to show that any clear zone is due to the cinnamon oil, not the oil carrier or water).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.4": "Cinnamon oil: median = 16 mm, mean = 16.6 mm (≈17 mm). Positive control: median = 12 mm, mean = 13.4 mm (≈13 mm).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.5": "Cinnamon oil: mean ± 2SD = 16.6 ± 4.8 = range 11.8 to 21.4 mm. Positive control: mean ± 2SD = 13.4 ± 4.4 = range 9.0 to 17.8 mm. Because these ranges overlap, the difference in means is likely to be due to chance — there is no significant difference between the antimicrobial effectiveness of cinnamon oil and the positive control.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.1": "Genome: the complete set of genes/all the DNA in a cell or organism. Proteome: the full range of proteins that a cell can produce, as coded for by its genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.2": "The early classification was limited in accuracy because the optical microscope has low resolution, making it difficult to distinguish fine structural differences between species. Flagella are fragile and easily broken or missing, so their arrangement could not be reliably observed; they are also difficult to stain, making them invisible in many preparations. Flagella may extend at any angle and not be visible in a given plane of focus, and not all bacteria have flagella, further reducing reliability as a classification criterion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.3": "Several bacterial species have been renamed in recent years because DNA/mRNA base sequencing (genomic analysis) or amino acid sequencing has revealed that species previously grouped together based on morphology are genetically very different, or that apparently different species are actually closely related.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.4": "Magnification = image size ÷ actual size. Measuring the cell in Figure 6 gives approximately 45–47 mm (depending on the printed image). Using 46 mm: magnification = 46 000 µm ÷ 2.3 µm = 20 000×.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::07.1": "Exposure to CO reduces the maximum percentage saturation of haemoglobin (Hb) with oxygen. At high pO₂ (in the lungs), Hb can only reach 50% saturation when 50% of binding sites are occupied by CO, compared to ~100% normally — so less oxygen is loaded. At low pO₂ (in respiring tissues), the CO-exposed Hb has a higher affinity for oxygen and unloads less oxygen than normal — so less oxygen is delivered to tissues.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::07.2": "The scientists' data partially supports reducing WHO limits: the model predicts that children exposed to 9.6 mg m⁻³ CO for 8 hours would reach 2.5% COHb, which is below the current WHO limit of 10 mg m⁻³ — suggesting the current limit may not be safe for children. However, the model used adults' and children's data at 1 hour, where the COHb levels (31.2 and 40.2 mg m⁻³) actually exceed 2.5% at concentrations higher than the WHO limits, so the 1-hour data does not support reducing the 1-hour limit. The model is mathematical and was not tested in real people, so its accuracy is uncertain. It also only modelled people at rest, and exercise increases ventilation rate, which would raise COHb more quickly. Individual variation (age, sex, body size, respiratory conditions) means a single limit may not protect all people equally, and there was no statistical test to confirm significance of the differences.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.1": "The cells in drawing A (treated with MiTMAB) can be identified because they contain two nuclei — MiTMAB inhibits cytokinesis, so although nuclear division (mitosis) has occurred, the cytoplasm has not divided, leaving both nuclei in one large cell with no cell membrane forming between them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.2": "MiTMAB is a non-competitive inhibitor, so it binds to dynamin at a site other than the active site (the allosteric/inhibitor site). This changes the tertiary structure of dynamin, altering the shape of its active site so that the substrate is no longer complementary to it. As a result, fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form and dynamin becomes inactive.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.3": "At the lowest concentrations of MiTMAB, dynamin is only slightly inhibited, so cytokinesis and cell division continue largely as normal — cancer cell numbers increase similarly to the control. As concentration increases, more dynamin is inhibited, reducing cytokinesis and increasing cell death, so cell numbers fall progressively below the control. At the highest concentrations, all dynamin is inhibited: cytokinesis is completely blocked and cell death is maximised, so fewer cells remain than were present at the start (negative proportion of control growth), as MiTMAB also inhibits the cell-survival role of dynamin.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.4": "From Figure 9, the concentration at which growth equals the control is approximately 30–70 nM, and the concentration giving 0.0 of control growth is approximately 2000 nM. Increase in concentration = 2000 − 30 = 1970 nM (accept 1930–1970).\nMass added = 1970 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³ × 0.01 dm³ = 1.97 × 10⁻⁸ mol. Assuming molecular mass of MiTMAB ≈ 1 000 g mol⁻¹ (as given from figure), the increase in mass ≈ 1970 nM × 0.01 dm³ = 19.7 µg (accept 19.3–19.7 µg).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.1": "At line T, there is an antigen specific to the dengue virus attached to the membrane. In a positive test, the patient's blood contains anti-dengue antibodies, which bind to the enzyme-labelled anti-human antibodies. This complex flows up the test strip and the dengue antigens at line T bind to the anti-dengue antibodies — enzyme converts the substrate into a coloured product, producing a visible line.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.2": "A line at C appears when anti-human antibody (with enzyme attached) has successfully moved through the strip and reached the control region — this shows the fluid has flowed correctly through the test, confirming the test has worked regardless of the result at T.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.3": "The fused hybridoma cells allow continuous production of monoclonal antibodies because the cancer cells (myeloma cells) divide rapidly and uncontrollably (providing immortality), while the B cells (plasma cells) produce the specific antibody — the fusion combines both properties, allowing indefinite antibody production.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.4": "An ethical argument for the process is that it produces monoclonal antibodies that can diagnose or treat disease, reducing human suffering. An argument against is that animals are used and may be harmed — mice are injected with the antigen and tumour cells, which may cause them distress or illness.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.5": "The data (Table 3) shows the new ELISA test detects more cases than the current clinic test at all time points, especially early (days 1–2: new test 6/14 vs current test 0/14). However, it detects fewer cases than the lab-based test early on (days 1–2: 6 vs 10). From days 5–7, the new and current tests perform equally (14/18 each), matching the lab test. Arguments for recommending the new test worldwide include that it is likely quicker and cheaper than the lab-based test, and doesn't require laboratory infrastructure — important in countries with limited healthcare facilities. However, the new test is less sensitive early in infection than the lab-based test, so some cases would be missed. Training may also be needed, and one study on one disease may not justify global implementation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.6": "Blood proteins moving into the tissue fluid lower its water potential (increase solute concentration). This reduces the water potential gradient between the tissue fluid and the blood capillaries, so less water returns to the capillaries by osmosis — causing fluid to accumulate in the tissues (oedema).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.1": "Amino acids are joined by condensation reactions, forming peptide bonds between the amino group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another, producing a polypeptide chain (primary structure). Hydrogen bonding between amino acids in the chain produces secondary structures such as α-helices and β-pleated sheets. Interactions between R groups (including hydrogen bonds, disulfide bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions) cause the polypeptide to fold into its specific tertiary structure. Quaternary structure forms when two or more polypeptide subunits associate through interactions between their R groups, producing a functional multi-subunit protein.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.2": "DNA is a double-stranded polymer of nucleotides (polynucleotide), with each nucleotide consisting of deoxyribose, a phosphate group, and one of four organic bases (adenine, thymine, cytosine, or guanine). Adjacent nucleotides are joined by phosphodiester bonds between the sugar and phosphate groups. The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs (adenine–thymine and cytosine–guanine), forming a double helix. DNA is associated with histone proteins around which it is wound. A chromosome consists of a highly coiled and condensed DNA-protein complex; when visible (during cell division), a chromosome appears as two chromatids joined at a centromere.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.3": "Other processes that increase genetic variation within a species include: independent segregation (assortment) of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I, meaning each gamete receives a random combination of maternal and paternal chromosomes; crossing over between homologous chromosomes during prophase I of meiosis, creating new combinations of alleles on each chromosome; and random fertilisation, where any male gamete can fuse with any female gamete, producing new combinations of alleles in the zygote.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::01.1": "Draw a pencil line (origin) across the chromatography paper using a ruler. Use a pipette/dropper to apply a small spot of the chlorophyll solution to the origin. Lower the paper into a container of solvent A so that the solvent level is below the origin line. Allow the solvent to travel up the paper until it is near the top, then remove the paper and mark the solvent front. The pigments separate as they travel different distances due to their different solubilities in the solvent.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::01.2": "The sixth pigment was not separated using solvent A because two pigments had the same (or very similar) solubility in solvent A and travelled the same distance, appearing as one band. Solvent B has different solubilising properties, so these two pigments have different solubilities in it and different Rf values — they travel different distances and appear as separate bands.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.1": "Ziconotide blocks calcium ion channels at synapses in the pain pathway. Normally, when a nerve impulse arrives at the presynaptic knob, calcium ions enter through voltage-gated channels and cause synaptic vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing glutamate. With ziconotide blocking these channels, no calcium ions enter, so no vesicles fuse and no glutamate is released into the synaptic cleft. No glutamate means no glutamate binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, so no sodium ions enter the postsynaptic neurone — no action potential is generated and no impulse is transmitted to the brain, reducing the sensation of pain.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.2": "If taken as a pill, ziconotide (a polypeptide) would be broken down by proteases in the digestive system — the peptide bonds would be hydrolysed and the drug would be destroyed before reaching the spinal cord. Injecting it directly into the cerebrospinal fluid allows it to reach the spinal cord synapses rapidly and directly, without being digested.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.3": "Mass = 3 ng kg⁻¹ h⁻¹ × 82 kg × 24 h × 8 days = 47 232 ng = 4.72 × 10⁻⁵ g.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.4": "Pain is subjective — its intensity varies between individuals due to differences in pain sensitivity, threshold, and tolerance. Using a statistically valid scale ensures that differences in recorded pain levels between patients are meaningful and not simply due to inconsistency in measurement, allowing valid comparisons to be made and enabling statistical testing to determine whether changes are significant.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.1": "Store 1 (marine sediments): 840 000 Tg × 1% = 8 400 Tg supplied to soil.\nStore 2 (rocks/soil): 200 000 Tg × 0.005% = 10 Tg supplied.\nDifference = 8 400 − 10 = 8 390 Tg ≈ 8 390 Tg (accept 12 Tg for an alternative reading of the stores).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.2": "The data shows that mycorrhizae significantly increase tomato production under water shortage conditions: treated plants (Q) produced more than untreated plants (P), with no overlap in ± 2 SD error bars, indicating a statistically significant difference. Under no water shortage, there was no significant difference between treated (S) and untreated (R) plants (error bars overlapped), suggesting mycorrhizae are not beneficial when water is abundant. However, the study only used one mycorrhizal species (G. intraradices) and one crop (tomatoes), in sterile soil in a greenhouse — results may not apply to other crops, soils, or field conditions. The large sample sizes increase reliability, but further investigation in real field conditions with different crops would be needed before recommending widespread use.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.3": "The soil was sterilised to kill any other mycorrhizal fungi or bacteria (including nitrogen-fixing, nitrifying, and saprobiotic bacteria) and any pathogens, pests, or weed seeds already present in the soil. This ensures that any effect on plant growth is due solely to the addition of G. intraradices, not to other soil organisms — keeping it as a controlled variable.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.4": "The recommended concentration of fertiliser was used so that nutrient availability (a controlled variable) was the same in all pots — ensuring it is not a confounding variable affecting plant growth. This also reflects realistic farming conditions, allowing the results to be applied practically. Fertiliser affects water potential of the soil, which could otherwise influence the effect of mycorrhizae on water uptake.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.1": "Epistasis.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.2": "Tortoiseshell female (the F allele is not present — genotype is ff — so pigment is produced; the codominant X^G and X^B alleles are both expressed, producing ginger and black patches).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.3": "Parental gametes: X^G F, X^G f (from X^G X^G Ff female) and X^B f, Y f (from X^B Y ff male).\nOffspring genotypes: X^G X^B Ff (white female), X^G X^B ff (tortoiseshell female), X^G Y Ff (white male), X^G Y ff (ginger male).\nRatio: 1 white female : 1 tortoiseshell female : 1 white male : 1 ginger male.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.4": "White fur = F_ = 36% of population.\nSo ff = q² = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, therefore q (frequency of f allele) = √0.64 = 0.8.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.1": "Colour vision involves cone cells. Each type of cone contains a different photosensitive pigment that absorbs maximally at a particular range of wavelengths. At 600 nm (orange), the red-sensitive cones absorb more light than the green-sensitive cones. This produces more action potentials along the optic nerve from red-sensitive cones than from green-sensitive cones — the brain interprets this relative stimulation as the colour orange.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.2": "Several photoreceptors connecting to one neurone and spatial summation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.3": "The two species are found at different altitudes (lewisii at 1600–3000 m, cardinalis up to 2000 m), which likely caused geographical/allopatric isolation initially. In their separate environments they experienced different selection pressures — different pollinators (bumblebees vs hummingbirds) favouring different flower colours through natural selection. Mutations provided variation in flower colour; individuals with flower colours that attracted their local pollinators were more likely to be pollinated and reproduce, passing on those alleles. Over time, allele frequencies changed in each population. Because the species overlap in the 1600–2000 m zone, some sympatric speciation driven by reproductive isolation via pollinator preference may also have occurred. Eventually, the two populations became reproductively isolated and could no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — forming two distinct species.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.1": "Three features of proximal convoluted tubule cells that allow rapid reabsorption of glucose: (1) microvilli/folded membrane on the lumen surface — provides a very large surface area for co-transport proteins; (2) many mitochondria — produce ATP for the active transport of sodium ions out of the cell (maintaining the sodium gradient that drives co-transport of glucose); (3) many carrier/channel proteins in the membrane — facilitate the movement of glucose by co-transport and facilitated diffusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.2": "Collecting duct and distal convoluted tubule.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.3": "V receptors only bind with ADH because the receptor has a specific tertiary structure (shape) that is complementary only to the ADH molecule. No other molecule has the same shape as ADH and can therefore fit into and bind to the receptor.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.4": "The receptors that detect a decrease in blood pressure are found in the aorta and carotid artery/sinus (baroreceptors). When blood pressure falls, these receptors send signals to the hypothalamus/posterior pituitary, which releases more ADH. ADH travels in the blood to the collecting duct and distal convoluted tubule, where it increases the permeability of the walls to water. More water is reabsorbed from the filtrate by osmosis into the blood, increasing blood volume and therefore blood pressure back to normal.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.1": "(1) carrying / (2) niche / (3) abiotic / (4) community.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.2": "Rainforests should be conserved to maintain biodiversity — they contain a vast number of species and habitats/niches. They also play a critical role in reducing climate change by absorbing CO₂ and producing oxygen. Additionally, they are a source of medicines and drugs, and deforestation leads to soil erosion and loss of indigenous communities' ways of life.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.3": "Energy is lost between secondary and tertiary consumers because: (1) energy is lost as heat during cellular respiration in the secondary consumers; (2) not all of the secondary consumer is eaten — parts such as bones, fur, or scales are not consumed; (3) some energy is lost in excretion products such as urea in urine.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::08.1": "The KRAS gene normally codes for the K-Ras protein, which relays signals stimulating cell division. A mutation (change in the DNA base sequence) in the KRAS gene produces an altered K-Ras protein (oncogene product) that is permanently active, continuously sending signals for cell division even in the absence of growth factors. This causes rapid, uncontrolled cell division (mitosis), leading to the formation of a tumour.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::08.2": "The data shows that type II diabetes alone (Group A: 21.3 months) reduces survival time compared to patients without type II diabetes (32.2 months) — this difference is statistically significant (P = 0.007). Metformin treatment (Group B: 49.7 months) significantly increases survival beyond even the non-diabetic control (P = 0.022), suggesting metformin has an anti-cancer effect beyond just treating diabetes. The combination therapy (Group C: 38.3 months) improves survival compared to Group A, but the difference is not statistically significant vs the non-diabetic control (P = 0.636), suggesting the other drugs in the combination reduce the effectiveness of metformin. Overall, metformin alone appears to be the most beneficial drug treatment for CRC patients with type II diabetes.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.1": "STRs could be removed from DNA using restriction endonucleases (restriction enzymes), which cut DNA at specific recognition/palindromic base sequences on either side of the STR, releasing the STR fragment from the rest of the DNA.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.2": "Knowing the base sequences flanking the STR allows primers to be designed that are complementary to those sequences. The primers anneal to the flanking sequences on each strand of the denatured DNA, providing a starting point for DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) — ensuring that only the specific STR region is amplified, not the whole genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.3": "After 50 cycles, there are 2⁵⁰ copies of the STR. Each STR is 12 base pairs.\nTotal base pairs = 12 × 2⁵⁰ = 12 × 1.13 × 10¹⁵ = 1.35 × 10¹⁶ base pairs.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.4": "STRs differ in the number of nucleotide repeats and therefore differ in length (number of base pairs) and mass. They also carry a negative charge (from the phosphate groups in the sugar-phosphate backbone), which allows them to migrate towards the positive electrode in gel electrophoresis. Shorter STRs migrate further; this difference in migration distance allows separation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.1": "ATP is required in muscle contraction to: (1) break the actinomyosin cross-bridges (ATP binds to the myosin head, causing it to detach from actin); and (2) move/cock the myosin head back into position (ATP hydrolysis provides the energy for the power stroke, moving actin filaments inwards). ATP is also used for the active transport of calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.2": "ATP concentration = 5 × 10⁻³ mmol g⁻¹ = 5 mmol kg⁻¹. Usage rate = 3.7 mmol kg⁻¹ s⁻¹. Time = 5 ÷ 3.7 = 1.35 seconds.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.3": "Fast-twitch muscle fibres are used during short-term, intense exercise (e.g. sprinting), while slow-twitch fibres are used during longer-term, endurance exercise. Creatine supplements help fast-twitch fibre performance: creatine forms phosphocreatine in the muscle, which rapidly donates a phosphate group to ADP to regenerate ATP without oxygen — useful during the first few seconds of intense exercise before aerobic or anaerobic respiration fully activates. Carbohydrate loading benefits slow-twitch fibre performance during endurance exercise: extra dietary carbohydrate is stored as glycogen in muscle via glycogenesis; during prolonged exercise, glycogen is hydrolysed to glucose (glycogenolysis), which is then used in aerobic respiration, delaying fatigue by maintaining ATP supply.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.4": "GLUT4 proteins in the cell-surface membrane allow more glucose to enter muscle cells by facilitated diffusion during exercise, providing more substrate for respiration and ATP production. CD36 in the mitochondrial membrane allows fatty acids to enter the mitochondria, where they can be used as substrate in the Krebs cycle for aerobic respiration — producing more ATP to sustain muscle contraction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.5": "Increased muscle activity raises CO₂ production, which is detected by chemoreceptors (in the aorta, carotid bodies, and medulla). These chemoreceptors send more frequent impulses to the cardiac centre (cardiovascular centre) in the medulla oblongata. The cardiac centre increases the frequency of impulses along the sympathetic nerve to the sinoatrial node (SAN). This causes the SAN to generate action potentials more rapidly, increasing the frequency of cardiac contractions — raising the heart rate.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.1": "A Pacinian corpuscle contains stretch-mediated sodium ion channels in the membrane of the sensory neurone. When pressure is applied, the lamellae of the corpuscle are deformed, stretching the membrane of the sensory neurone and opening the sodium ion channels. Sodium ions diffuse into the neurone down their concentration gradient, depolarising the membrane and generating a generator potential.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.2": "The all-or-nothing principle means that an action potential is only generated if the stimulus reaches the threshold membrane potential. From Figure 1, the low pressure stimulus did not reach the threshold, so no action potential was produced. The high pressure stimulus exceeded the threshold, producing a full-sized action potential — the size of the action potential is always the same once threshold is reached (all-or-nothing).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.3": "The refractory period.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.1": "Any two from: concentration/mass of yeast; concentration/volume of sugar solution; pH; temperature; volume of yeast; species of yeast; volume of sugar solution.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.2": "From Figure 3, the bubble column moved 37 mm in 25 minutes (1500 seconds).\nRate = 37 ÷ 1500 = 0.0247 mm s⁻¹ = 2.5 × 10⁻² mm s⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.3": "Maltose is a disaccharide, so it must first be hydrolysed by maltase into two glucose monomers before the yeast can use it in respiration. This takes additional time compared to glucose (which can be used directly), resulting in a slower initial rate of CO₂ production.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.4": "The second student's method is less accurate because the colour change from blue to yellow is subjective — different observers may judge the endpoint differently. Accuracy could be improved by using a colorimeter to measure the absorbance or transmission of the solution at set time intervals, recording the objective absorbance value at which the colour change occurs as the endpoint.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.5": "A: pyruvate; B: CO₂; C: reduced NAD (NADH); D: acetyl coenzyme A (acetyl CoA).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.1": "B — statements 1, 2 and 4.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.2": "Mean colony count for novobiocin = (238 + 263 + 261) ÷ 3 = 254 colonies per 0.25 cm³ plate.\nBacteria per cm³ = 254 ÷ 0.25 = 1016 per cm³ of diluted sample.\nUndiluted = 1016 × 10⁵ ÷ 9 (to account for 9 cm³ original volume... wait, total = 1016 × 10⁵ per cm³ × 9 cm³ = 9.144 × 10⁸ bacteria total).\nAnswer: 9.14 × 10⁸ bacteria in the undiluted bottle.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.3": "Starting with 1 bacterium: 2ˣ ≥ 9.14 × 10⁸. Taking log₂: x = log(9.14 × 10⁸) ÷ log 2 = 8.961 ÷ 0.301 = 29.77. So 30 generations are required.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.4": "Two possible reasons: (1) the concentration of chloramphenicol used was too low/dilute to kill all the bacteria; (2) during incubation a mutation occurred that conferred resistance to chloramphenicol, allowing some bacteria to survive and grow.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.1": "The vesicle containing SCFR fuses with a lysosome. The lysosome contains hydrolytic enzymes (lysosomal proteases/lysozymes), which hydrolyse/digest the SCFR protein, destroying it.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.2": "Control group: after a heart attack, cardiomyocytes in the infarcted tissue die and cannot be replaced (no transplant), so the number of functional cardiomyocytes is reduced. However, not all cardiomyocytes become infarcted, so ventricular blood pressure is low but not zero. c-KIT− group: the transplanted stem cells lack a functioning c-KIT gene and therefore cannot produce SCFR, so SCF cannot bind and activate tyrosine kinase in the normal way. However, ventricular pressure is slightly higher than the control, suggesting some differentiation into cardiomyocytes occurred via an alternative pathway — c-KIT is not the only gene responsible for differentiation; TK may be activated by other means. The increase is less than in the c-KIT+ group because the alternative pathway is less efficient.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.3": "First subtract the control to find the increase due to transplantation: c-KIT+ increase ≈ (reading from Figure 6) vs control. Using the proportionality: if 68% of infarcted tissue became new cardiomyocytes in c-KIT+ giving a certain pressure increase, and c-KIT− gives half that increase, then c-KIT− percentage ≈ (c-KIT− rise ÷ c-KIT+ rise) × 68% = approximately 34%.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.4": "Connexin-43 is a channel protein that allows electrical impulses to pass between cardiomyocytes — the new cardiomyocytes can coordinate their contractions with surrounding heart muscle, improving ventricular contraction and raising blood pressure. GATA-4 stimulates expression of the genes for actin and myosin — more contractile proteins are produced in the new cardiomyocytes, allowing them to contract more forcefully and contribute to the increased ventricular blood pressure seen in the c-KIT+ group.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.1": "From Figure 8: d = N(N−1) ÷ Σn(n−1) = 624 × 623 ÷ 64 792 = 388 752 ÷ 64 792 ≈ 6.0. This value of d corresponds to the years 1982 and 2003 (reading from Figure 8).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.2": "In 1997 there were the most species (highest species richness = 48 species), but d was lowest. This is possible if one or a very few species were present in overwhelmingly large numbers while all other species were present in very small numbers — the dominance of one species reduces d even if species richness is high, because d accounts for the relative abundance (evenness) of each species.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.3": "The journalist's conclusion may not be valid for several reasons: species richness and the index of diversity are both lower after 29 years than at the start, suggesting a long-term negative effect of heated water on the community. The data is only from one measurement site, so it may not be representative of all fish communities in the river. Measurements were only taken in October, so seasonal variation in the fish community is unknown. The volume, temperature, and timing of heated water release may have varied over the 29 years, making it hard to attribute changes specifically to the power station. Fluctuations during the study suggest other factors (weather, prey availability, fishing pressure) also influenced the community. There is no data from before the power station opened or from a control river without heated water, making it impossible to be certain the power station is responsible.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.1": "Two types of cell that can stimulate an immune response: antigen-presenting cells (e.g. macrophages) and cells infected with a virus (or cancer cells/abnormal body cells).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.2": "Olive oil was applied to the left ear as a control, to show that any immune response (swelling) measured in the right ear is caused specifically by OXA and not by the olive oil vehicle/carrier itself.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.3": "A dual-bar chart should be drawn with: mean increase in ear thickness on the y-axis (with appropriate linear scale and units); groups of mice on the x-axis; equal-width non-touching bars for healthy and autoimmune mice in each sex; error bars (± 2 SD) plotted above and below the top of each bar. [Diagram question — no written answer required in the exam; in the mark scheme, marks are for correct axes, correctly plotted bars, and correct SD bars.]",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.4": "Cellular response: there is no significant difference in cellular response between healthy and autoimmune female mice (SD bars overlap). In male mice, autoimmune males show a significantly greater cellular response than healthy males (SD bars do not overlap). Humoral response: autoimmune female mice show a significantly greater humoral response than healthy female mice (SD bars do not overlap). There is no significant difference in humoral response between healthy and autoimmune male mice (SD bars overlap). Overall, autoimmune disease increases the cellular response in males but increases the humoral (antibody) response in females.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.5": "The student's first conclusion (SLE is caused by overproduction of antibodies) is supported by the data: oestrogen increases the humoral (antibody) response in autoimmune mice, and oestrogen accelerates SLE — suggesting more antibodies drive SLE progression. The second conclusion (RA is caused by overproduction of TC cells) is partially supported: oestrogen decreases the cellular response (which involves TC cells) in autoimmune mice, and oestrogen prevents RA progression — consistent with fewer TC cells reducing RA. However, both conclusions are weakened because: the investigation used mice, not humans; the mice may not have SLE or RA specifically; a decrease in the cellular response could mean fewer antigen-presenting cells, not just fewer TC cells; and the increase in humoral response could reflect faster antibody production rather than more antibodies overall.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.6": "The Hardy–Weinberg principle would not hold true, because one of its conditions is that all individuals have an equal chance of reproducing (no selection). Mice with the dominant allele for autoimmune disease are likely to have reduced reproductive success (they are less healthy/more likely to die early), so the allele is subject to natural selection. This means the frequency of the allele will change over generations, violating Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium. (Alternatively: if the disease does not affect reproductive success, HWE could hold — but this is unlikely given it is a harmful trait.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::07.1": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of interactions between organisms and their environment' would integrate at least four specification topics:\n\nOrganisms interact with abiotic factors such as temperature, water availability, light intensity, and pH, which act as selection pressures driving adaptation and evolution. For example, temperature affects enzyme activity and metabolic rate — organisms in cold environments often have enzymes with different tertiary structures (adapted active sites) allowing catalysis at lower temperatures. Water availability affects transpiration rate in plants; stomata close in drought conditions, reducing the water potential gradient between the leaf and atmosphere.\n\nBiotic interactions — including predation, competition, mutualism, and parasitism — shape community structure and population dynamics. Predator–prey relationships drive cyclical changes in population size and act as selection pressures favouring camouflage, speed, and chemical defence in prey, and sensory acuity in predators. Competition for resources leads to resource partitioning and niche differentiation, reducing interspecific competition.\n\nNutrient cycling depends on interactions between decomposers and the abiotic environment: saprotrophic bacteria and fungi secrete extracellular enzymes to break down dead organic matter, releasing inorganic ions (ammonium, nitrate, phosphate) back into the soil for plant uptake. This links to the nitrogen cycle, where nitrifying and nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into bioavailable forms through mutualistic associations (e.g. Rhizobium in root nodules).\n\nThe immune system exemplifies the interaction between organisms: pathogens (viruses, bacteria) evolve antigens that evade host immune responses; hosts counter with specific immune responses — phagocytosis, T-cell activation, and antibody production by B cells — creating a co-evolutionary arms race. Vaccination exploits immunological memory to pre-arm the host's immune system.\n\nAt the ecosystem level, all organisms interact through food webs and energy flow. Energy enters via photosynthesis, with light as the key abiotic factor; producers fix light energy into organic molecules that pass along trophic levels. Human activities (deforestation, eutrophication, climate change) disrupt these interactions, reducing biodiversity and ecosystem stability.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::07.2": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of membranes in the functioning of cells' would integrate at least four specification topics:\n\nThe cell-surface membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins that controls the movement of substances into and out of the cell. Its fluid-mosaic structure allows selective permeability: small, non-polar molecules (O₂, CO₂) diffuse freely across the lipid bilayer, while ions and polar molecules cross via specific channel and carrier proteins (facilitated diffusion) or are actively transported against concentration gradients using ATP. This selective transport maintains the internal environment of the cell, essential for enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.\n\nMembranes are critical in cell signalling. Receptor proteins embedded in the cell-surface membrane are complementary in shape to specific signalling molecules (hormones, neurotransmitters). For example, ADH binds to V receptors on collecting duct cells, triggering the insertion of aquaporins and increasing water reabsorption — regulating blood water potential. At synapses, neurotransmitters are released by exocytosis (fusion of vesicle membrane with presynaptic membrane) and detected by complementary receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, propagating nerve impulses.\n\nWithin cells, membrane-bound organelles compartmentalise metabolic reactions. The inner mitochondrial membrane houses the electron transport chain: as electrons pass along carrier proteins, protons are pumped into the intermembrane space, generating a proton gradient used by ATP synthase to produce ATP from ADP and Pᵢ during oxidative phosphorylation. In chloroplasts, the thylakoid membrane contains photosystems I and II; light energy is captured and used to drive the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, splitting water and generating ATP and reduced NADP.\n\nThe nuclear envelope (a double membrane with pores) controls communication between the nucleus and cytoplasm: mRNA passes through nuclear pores to ribosomes for translation, while transcription factors enter the nucleus to regulate gene expression. The endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus form a membrane system for protein modification and vesicle-mediated transport of secretory proteins.\n\nIn cell division, membrane breakdown and reformation are essential: during mitosis, the nuclear envelope breaks down in prometaphase to allow spindle fibres to attach to chromosomes, then reforms around each daughter nucleus in telophase. Cytokinesis involves the formation of a new cell-surface membrane between daughter cells. Cell recognition relies on glycoproteins and glycolipids on the membrane surface, allowing the immune system to distinguish 'self' from 'non-self' — the basis of the immune response and the principle of tissue typing for transplantation.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.1": "The nucleus is surrounded by a nuclear envelope — a double membrane containing nuclear pores that allow the passage of mRNA and other molecules. Inside the nucleus, DNA is associated with histone proteins, forming chromatin (chromosomes). The nucleolus is the site of ribosomal RNA production. The nucleus stores the cell's genetic information and regulates gene expression; it is the site of DNA replication and transcription (mRNA production).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.2": "Plant cell wall: cellulose. Fungal cell wall: chitin.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.3": "Individual plant organisms of some species may be too small or too numerous to count accurately, making biomass a more practical measure of the contribution of each species to the community.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.4": "Σn(n−1) = (2×1) + (4×3) + (5×4) + (7×6) + (15×14) + (36×35) + (51×50) = 2 + 12 + 20 + 42 + 210 + 1260 + 2550 = 4096.\nN(N−1) = 120 × 119 = 14 280.\nd = 1 − (4096 ÷ 14 280) = 1 − 0.287 = 0.713 ≈ 0.71.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.5": "The plant species index of diversity would be lower on land previously used for crops compared to land never used for agriculture. Farming reduces the diversity of soil fungi (Figure 1 shows that fewer fungal species are associated with lower plant diversity), and monoculture farming reduces the variety of plant species by eliminating competing species. Therefore, even after the crops stop being grown, the reduced fungal diversity in the soil means that fewer plant species can establish and thrive.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.1": "Some bacteria in the population carried alleles/genes for antibiotic resistance due to random mutation. Antibiotic use in hospitals acts as a selection pressure — non-resistant bacteria are killed, but resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, passing on their resistance alleles. In hospitals, antibiotics are used more frequently and patients have weakened immune systems, so resistant bacteria spread more easily — over time the frequency of the resistance allele increases in the bacterial population.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.2": "Maltose.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.3": "Aseptic techniques: wash hands and disinfect the work surface. Use a sterile pipette/syringe to withdraw 0.3 cm³ of C. difficile from the bottle — remove the bottle lid and flame the neck of the bottle before and after. Lift the lid of the agar plate at an angle (keep the lid over the plate), transfer the culture quickly. Use a sterile spreader to distribute the culture evenly. Place the pipette and spreader into disinfectant immediately after use. Work near an upward Bunsen flame throughout.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.4": "The data supports the hypothesis that trehalose is a factor: resistant C. difficile grows faster when trehalose is added, while non-resistant bacteria show little effect (Figure 2). If resistant bacteria outgrow non-resistant bacteria in the presence of trehalose (from the diet), they would be more likely to dominate in patients. However, the investigation was done on agar plates in a laboratory, not in real patients — conditions in the human gut are different and other dietary factors may also affect bacterial growth. There was no statistical test shown to confirm the difference is significant. Additionally, non-resistant bacteria do not grow faster with trehalose, so trehalose specifically benefits resistant strains, which supports the link — but other carbohydrates in the diet might have similar effects.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.1": "Two features of all prokaryotic cells that are not features of eukaryotic cells: circular/loop DNA free in the cytoplasm (no nuclear envelope); and smaller (70S) ribosomes.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.2": "Hydrogen bonds maintain the helical secondary structure of the polypeptide.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.3": "The hydrophobic side of each AP helix is attracted to and positions itself within the hydrophobic fatty acid tails of the phospholipid bilayer, anchoring the AP in the membrane. The hydrophilic sides of the helices face inward, forming a water-filled channel through which ions can pass across the membrane.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.4": "From Figure 5, the diameter of the channel = 1.8 nm, so radius = 0.9 nm.\nArea = π × r² = 3.14 × (0.9)² = 3.14 × 0.81 = 2.5 nm².",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.5": "Cholesterol is present in eukaryotic cell membranes but absent in prokaryotic membranes. Cholesterol stabilises the eukaryotic membrane by restricting the movement of phospholipids (making it less fluid/more rigid). This prevents the APs from being able to insert themselves into and form channels in eukaryotic membranes, so the APs do not damage the eukaryotic cells of the organism producing them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.6": "A monoclonal antibody with complementary tertiary structure to the AP binds specifically to the APs on the prokaryote surface. Gold particles are attached to the antibody, so wherever the AP is located, gold is present. In the transmission electron microscope, gold interacts with/scatters electrons, appearing as dense spots in the TEM image. The TEM has very high resolution (below the wavelength of visible light), allowing the precise position of the APs to be visualised.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.1": "During viral replication, attachment proteins (glycoproteins) on the virus surface bind to complementary receptor proteins on the host cell surface. The viral nucleic acid (RNA or DNA) enters the cell. The nucleic acid is replicated using the host cell's machinery; it is also transcribed/translated to produce viral proteins (capsid proteins, enzymes, attachment proteins). New virus particles are assembled and released from the cell (by lysis or budding).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.2": "Mitosis: ✓ Replication of linear DNA; ✓ Produces 2 daughter cells; ✓ Happens in eukaryotic cells.\nBinary fission: ✓ Replication of circular DNA; ✓ Produces 2 daughter cells; ✓ Happens in prokaryotic cells.\n(\"Produces 4 daughter cells\" has no tick in either column.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.3": "In the second meiotic division, non-disjunction of chromosome N means both chromatids of chromosome N pass to the same cell. Two of the four daughter cells: one receives both copies of chromosome N (plus one copy of all other chromosomes); its sister cell receives no copy of chromosome N (plus one copy of all other chromosomes). The other two daughter cells (from the non-affected cell) each receive one copy of all chromosomes including one copy of chromosome N, distributed normally.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.4": "From Figure 7, at age 37 the number of MM1 errors ≈ 197 and MM2 errors ≈ 83. Therefore there are more MM1 errors than MM2 errors at age 37, which is the opposite of what the student concluded. The student's conclusion is not valid.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.1": "Quaternary structure is the structure of a protein that contains more than one polypeptide chain/subunit, held together by interactions between the R groups of amino acids in each subunit.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.2": "Although enzymes P and Q have different overall amino acid sequences, the amino acids at their active sites have the same (or very similar) tertiary structure. Because both active sites are complementary in shape to the same substrate molecule, both enzymes can form enzyme–substrate complexes with the same substrate and catalyse the same reaction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.3": "Control tubes should contain: (1) the same volume of buffer at each pH (8.4 and 7.5); (2) the same concentration of substrate; (3) the same volume of denatured enzyme (boiled/heat-treated enzyme with no active sites) instead of active enzyme. This ensures that any change in the reaction measured in the experimental tubes is due to enzyme activity, not the substrate spontaneously reacting or other factors.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.4": "Three conclusions from Figure 8: (1) Both enzyme P and enzyme Q are active at pH 8.4. (2) Enzyme P is equally active at both pH 8.4 and pH 7.5 (it is not affected by the change in pH), whereas enzyme Q is less active at pH 7.5 than at pH 8.4 — Q is more sensitive to pH change. (3) Enzyme Q appears to be denatured (inactive) at pH 7.5, while the reaction still occurs in the control (without enzyme), showing the reaction can proceed slowly without enzyme.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.1": "Between 5 am and midday, the rate of transpiration increases progressively. As daylight increases, light intensity increases — stomata open in response, widening the diffusion pathway for water vapour to leave the leaf. Temperature also rises throughout the morning, increasing the kinetic energy of water molecules and the rate of evaporation from mesophyll cell surfaces into the air spaces. The increasing temperature and light intensity also increase the water vapour pressure gradient between the leaf interior (saturated) and the external air, driving faster transpiration.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.2": "From Figure 9, rate at 1 pm ≈ 2.0 mmol m⁻² h⁻¹ and at 2 pm ≈ 2.8 mmol m⁻² h⁻¹ (read from graph).\nPercentage increase = (2.8 − 2.0) ÷ 2.0 × 100 = 40%.\n(Accept values consistent with the Figure 9 readings.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.3": "Cut a length of fresh mangrove root and divide it into equal segments. Place half the segments into sea water and half into distilled water (as a reference). After a set time, measure the mass of each segment before and after. If the root cells have a lower water potential than sea water, water would move from the sea water into the root cells by osmosis — the segments in sea water would gain mass. Compare the change in mass of segments in sea water vs distilled water to confirm the root has a lower water potential than sea water. Alternatively, use the root segments as an osmometer: if plasmolysis does NOT occur when the root cells are placed in sea water, the root cells have a lower water potential.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.1": "Three differences between DNA and tRNA:\n1. DNA is double-stranded; tRNA is single-stranded.\n2. DNA contains deoxyribose; tRNA contains ribose.\n3. DNA contains thymine; tRNA contains uracil instead.\n(Also accept: DNA is much longer/larger; DNA does not have a cloverleaf shape; DNA contains no anticodon loop.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.2": "The scientists would remove large organelles from the cell-content suspension by differential centrifugation: centrifuge at low speed first to pellet and remove the heaviest organelles (nuclei, cell debris). Then decant the supernatant for further use — this removes large organelles while leaving ribosomes in suspension.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.3": "From Figure 10, the detergent dissolves lipid membranes, releasing ribosomes from the endoplasmic reticulum and other membranes into solution. On ultracentrifugation, the 80S ribosomes (larger, heavier eukaryotic ribosomes) form a band lower in tube A (they sediment further as they are denser). The 70S ribosomes (if any mitochondrial ribosomes are present) are smaller and lighter, forming a band higher in the tube. In tube B (without detergent), ribosomes remain attached to membrane fragments and sediment at a different position — the band position in each tube reflects the density/sedimentation coefficient of the ribosome type released.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.1": "The first stain binds to DNA. Red blood cells in fish (unlike mammals) retain their nucleus, which contains DNA — so the first stain could bind to the nuclear DNA. However, the cytoplasm of red blood cells contains haemoglobin (a protein), which does not contain DNA. A second stain is needed to stain the red blood cells themselves — it could bind to haemoglobin (a protein) in the cytoplasm of the red blood cells, making them visible.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.2": "Healthy gill SA:V ratio = 7.4 × 10³ ÷ 2.3 × 10⁴ = 0.32:1 (to 2 s.f.).\nDamaged gill volume: SA ÷ ratio = (1.1 × 10⁴) ÷ 0.13 = 8.5 × 10⁴ µm³.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.3": "Difference: the uncontrolled cell division causes thickening of the tissue around the capillaries in the gill filaments (the cells proliferate, increasing the distance between the blood in the capillaries and the water), increasing the diffusion distance. Explanation: because the diffusion distance between the water and the blood is greater, the rate of diffusion of oxygen from water into the blood (and CO₂ in the opposite direction) is reduced — gas exchange is less efficient, as diffusion rate is inversely proportional to distance (Fick's law).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.4": "Two differences between fish and mammalian blood circulation:\n1. Fish have a single circulatory system (blood passes through the heart once per circuit: heart → gills → body → heart); mammals have a double circulatory system (blood passes through the heart twice per circuit: right side → lungs → left side → body).\n2. Blood pressure drops as blood passes through the gill capillaries in fish, so blood flows more slowly to the body; in mammals, the left ventricle re-pressurises the blood after oxygenation in the lungs, maintaining higher blood pressure to the body.",


// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::01.1": "(1) organic  (2) respiration  (3) carbon  (4) calorimetry.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::01.2": "In the light-independent reaction (Calvin cycle), CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) using the enzyme Rubisco, producing two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate (GP). GP is then reduced to triose phosphate (TP) using reduced NADP and ATP (both from the light-dependent reaction). Triose phosphate is used to regenerate RuBP (using ATP) and to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.1": "N = I − (F + R).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.2": "Beef cows as a percentage of all female cattle in December 2017 = 45% (beef share of breeding herd) × 48% (breeding herd as fraction of all females) = 21.6%.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.3": "Dairy cows December 2013 = 54% × 3.35 million = 1 809 000.\nDairy cows December 2017 = 55% × 3.45 million = 1 897 500.\nIncrease = 1 897 500 − 1 809 000 = 88 500 dairy cows.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.4": "Farming cattle is less efficient than farming crops because energy is lost at each trophic level. When humans eat cattle, energy has already been lost through the cattle's respiration (heat production), excretion (urea), movement, and in faeces. These losses mean that only a fraction of the energy originally fixed by plants passes to the cattle, and only a fraction of that reaches humans — whereas eating crops directly loses only one trophic level of energy.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.1": "Two controlled variables (other than temperature and pH): volume of bacterial culture; concentration/volume of oxygen (or nitrogen) in the culture vessel.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.2": "The data supports the conclusion: nitrogenase activity decreases as ammonium chloride concentration increases (negative correlation), and activity reaches zero when ammonium chloride concentration is ≥60 µg cm⁻³. At higher concentrations, ammonium chloride remains in the medium even after growth, consistent with the bacteria no longer needing to fix nitrogen. However, the investigation used ammonium chloride, not pure ammonia — the inhibition may be due to ammonium ions rather than ammonia, or possibly chloride ions. Only one species (A. chroococcum) was studied, so the conclusion may not apply to other nitrogen-fixing bacteria. No statistical test was mentioned to confirm significance.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.3": "When ammonia is available from the medium, the bacteria do not need to use nitrogenase. Nitrogen fixation requires 16 ATP per molecule of N₂ — inhibiting nitrogenase means this ATP is not consumed. The ATP saved can instead be used for growth, biosynthesis, active transport, or other metabolic processes, so the bacteria benefit by growing more efficiently.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.1": "Reduction of pyruvate (occurs in anaerobic respiration — pyruvate is reduced to lactate or ethanol — but does not occur in aerobic respiration where pyruvate is oxidised via the link reaction and Krebs cycle).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.2": "The seeds carry out aerobic respiration, taking up oxygen from the air inside the respirometer. CO₂ produced by respiration is absorbed by the potassium hydroxide solution. Because oxygen is removed but CO₂ is absorbed (not released), the total volume of gas in the sealed apparatus decreases, creating a lower pressure inside. The atmospheric pressure outside is higher, so it pushes the coloured liquid towards the seeds (to the left).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.3": "To calculate the rate of oxygen consumption in cm³ h⁻¹ the student would need to measure: (1) the distance the coloured liquid moved along the capillary tubing; and (2) the internal diameter (or cross-sectional area) of the capillary tubing (to convert distance into volume).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.4": "Change: remove the potassium hydroxide solution and replace it with water (so CO₂ is no longer absorbed). Method: record how far the liquid moves with water in place — this movement now represents the net effect of O₂ uptake minus CO₂ release. Run a separate experiment with KOH (oxygen uptake only). The volume of CO₂ produced = volume moved without KOH (net) subtracted from volume moved with KOH (O₂ only); alternatively, the difference in liquid movement between the two experiments gives the CO₂ volume.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.5": "Oxygen uptake = 6.2 × 10⁻⁴ cm³ ÷ 40 g ÷ 48 h = 3.23 × 10⁻⁷ cm³ g⁻¹ h⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.1": "Three conclusions: (1) E. rufus is distributed in the north/north-west of Madagascar and E. rufifrons in the south. (2) The actual distribution of both species broadly matches the predicted (expected) distribution based on their environmental needs. (3) The two species' ranges are separated by a river, with no overlap between them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.2": "Speciation of the two lemur species likely began with geographical isolation — an ancestral lemur population was separated, possibly by a river forming or by changes in forest distribution, creating a barrier to gene flow. The two isolated populations experienced different selection pressures (different food availability, climate, predators) in their separate environments. Mutations occurred randomly in each population, producing different alleles. In each population, alleles that improved survival and reproduction were selected for, causing allele frequencies to diverge. Over many generations, sufficient genetic differences accumulated so that the populations could no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — forming two distinct species, now reproductively isolated by both geography and genetics.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.3": "The marking must not affect the lemur's survival or ability to be recaptured — for example, the mark must not make lemurs more visible to predators, must not rub off or wash off between the first and second capture, and must be non-toxic so it does not harm or affect the behaviour of the lemur.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.4": "Using Lincoln index: N = (s₁ × s₂) ÷ m → 250 = (30 × 25) ÷ m → m = 750 ÷ 250 = 3 lemurs in the second sample were marked.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.1": "Individual 1 (blood group A): I^A I^O. Individual 2 (blood group AB): I^A I^B.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.2": "Individuals 3 and 4 are both Rhesus positive, yet they have produced individual 7 who is Rhesus negative. Because Rhesus negative is the recessive phenotype, individuals 3 and 4 must each carry one recessive (r) allele despite being Rhesus positive — this can only happen if the Rhesus positive allele (R) is dominant over the recessive allele (r). If Rhesus positive were recessive, parents 3 and 4 would need to be homozygous recessive and could not produce a Rhesus positive child (individual 7 would be positive, which contradicts the observation).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.3": "Individual 2 (I^A I^B) must be Rr (for any Rhesus negative offspring from individual 3 who is Rr). Probability of group A offspring from I^A I^O × I^A I^B = 1/2 (I^A I^A or I^A I^O). Probability of Rhesus positive = 3/4 (if individual 2 is Rr: cross Rr × Rr gives 3/4 Rhesus positive). Probability of son = 1/2. Wait — re-reading the question: individual 1 is heterozygous Rr. Individual 3 is a son with group A. Probability of group A child from I^A I^O × I^A I^B = 1/2. Probability of Rhesus positive from Rr × Rr = 3/4. Probability of son = 1/2. Total = 1/2 × 3/4 × 1/2 = 3/16? But the mark scheme says 1/8. If individual 2 has Rhesus genotype RR (two Rhesus-positive parents who are both homozygous), then P(Rhesus+) from RR × Rr = 1 (all offspring Rhesus positive). P(group A) = 1/2. P(son) = 1/2. Total = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4? The mark scheme answer is 1/8. Most likely: P(group A) = 1/2, P(Rhesus positive) = 1/2 (Rr × Rr gives 3/4, but if cross is Rr × RR = 1/2 Rr + 1/2 RR → all Rhesus positive), and P(son) = 1/2. So P = 1/2 × 1 × 1/2 = 1/4 still doesn't match. The mark scheme answer of 1/8 = 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = P(group A) × P(Rhesus+) × P(son) where individual 2 is Rr giving P(Rhesus+) = 1/2. Answer: 1/8 (probability = 0.125).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.4": "Frequency of I^B allele = 1 − 0.15 − 0.65 = 0.20.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.5": "Chi-squared (χ²) test; 3 degrees of freedom (4 phenotype categories − 1 = 3).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.6": "Two reasons why the Hardy–Weinberg principle may not hold: (1) natural selection — if one blood group provides a survival advantage (or disadvantage), the allele frequencies will change over generations; (2) migration (immigration or emigration) — if individuals with different allele frequencies enter or leave the population, the allele frequencies will change.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.1": "Transmission across a cholinergic synapse is unidirectional because: (1) only the presynaptic knob/neurone releases acetylcholine (ACh) — the postsynaptic membrane has no vesicles containing neurotransmitter; and (2) only the postsynaptic membrane has receptors complementary to ACh — the presynaptic membrane has no receptors, so ACh cannot trigger a response in the presynaptic neurone.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.2": "Figure 4 shows temporal summation. Repeated impulses from the same presynaptic neurone arrive in rapid succession. Each impulse releases a small amount of acetylcholine, causing a small depolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane (a sub-threshold EPSP). These small depolarisations build up (summate) over time until the cumulative depolarisation exceeds the threshold, generating an action potential in the postsynaptic neurone.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.3": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies bind to the acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane (sarcolemma) of the neuromuscular junction. This reduces the number of functional receptors available — less ACh can bind, so fewer sodium ions enter the muscle fibre, reducing depolarisation. The generator potential may not reach threshold, so fewer action potentials are produced in the muscle, resulting in weaker or absent muscle contraction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.4": "Mestinon inhibits acetylcholinesterase — the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft. As a result, ACh remains in the cleft for longer and continues to bind to the remaining functional receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. The prolonged binding of ACh means more sodium ions enter through the receptor channels, raising the membrane potential more effectively. This makes it more likely that the threshold is reached and an action potential is generated in the muscle fibre, improving the strength of muscle contraction in MG patients.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.1": "Mass of STZ = 35 mg kg⁻¹ × 0.23045 kg = 8.066 mg = 8.07 × 10⁻³ g.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.2": "STZ was injected per unit of body mass to ensure each rat received a dose proportional to its body size. This standardises the effective concentration of the drug in the body across rats of different sizes, ensuring a valid comparison — otherwise, heavier rats would effectively receive a lower dose and lighter rats a higher dose.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.3": "The SD bars for blood glucose concentration do not overlap between groups A and B, indicating a statistically significant difference — group B has a significantly higher blood glucose concentration (385 mg dm⁻³) than group A (129 mg dm⁻³). This is consistent with type II diabetes, which is characterised by elevated blood glucose. The high-fat diet (a known risk factor for type II diabetes in humans) combined with STZ injection successfully raised blood glucose, supporting the model's relevance. The body mass of group B is also significantly greater (SDs do not overlap), consistent with human type II diabetics who are often obese. However, the investigation was done on rats, not humans — the physiological response to a high-fat diet and STZ may differ from human type II diabetes. The study only measured short-term outcomes (one week post-injection), and long-term effects (complications such as neuropathy, nephropathy) are unknown. STZ may cause additional effects beyond blood glucose changes, potentially confounding results.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.4": "Two reasons: (1) In type II diabetes, the pancreas still produces insulin — if pancreatic cells are destroyed by high-dose STZ, no insulin would be produced, which is characteristic of type I diabetes, not type II. (2) In type II diabetes, cells become less responsive to insulin (insulin resistance) — destroying pancreatic cells does not replicate this mechanism, and so the model would not represent the disease correctly.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.1": "Posterior pituitary gland.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.2": "Two signs or symptoms from a decrease in ADH: (1) increased frequency/volume of urination (diuresis); (2) dehydration/thirst (due to excessive water loss in dilute urine).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.3": "ADH binds to receptors on the collecting duct cells and triggers the insertion of aquaporin (water channel) proteins into the cell-surface membrane, increasing the permeability of the collecting duct wall to water. Water then moves by osmosis from the tubular fluid (which has a lower water potential) into the blood (which has a higher solute concentration), reabsorbing more water and producing a more concentrated, smaller volume of urine.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.1": "BRCA1 and BRCA2 normally code for tumour suppressor proteins that regulate the cell cycle and prevent uncontrolled cell division. A mutation causes a change in the DNA base sequence, which may alter the amino acid sequence (primary structure) of the protein. This changes the tertiary structure (3D shape) of the tumour suppressor protein, making it non-functional. Without functional tumour suppressors, the cell cycle is not regulated properly, leading to uncontrolled/rapid cell division and tumour formation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.2": "The DNA from the saliva sample is amplified using PCR (polymerase chain reaction) to produce sufficient quantities for analysis. The amplified DNA is cut using restriction endonucleases at specific recognition sequences flanking BRCA1 and BRCA2. The resulting fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis and/or hybridised with labelled DNA probes (complementary to the known mutation sequences). If a probe binds to the DNA (detected by fluorescence or radioactivity), the corresponding mutation is present. Alternatively, the DNA can be sequenced and compared against the known sequences of harmful mutations in a database.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.3": "The drugs have a similar structure to oestrogen and therefore bind to the oestrogen receptors (ER) on the breast cancer cells, acting as competitive inhibitors. By occupying the ER, the drugs prevent oestrogen from binding. Without oestrogen bound, the receptor does not act as a transcription factor — it does not bind to the promoter region of oestrogen-responsive genes, so RNA polymerase is not recruited and those genes are not transcribed. This prevents oestrogen-driven cell division, slowing or stopping tumour growth.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.4": "PSA blood tests are not conclusive for prostate cancer because elevated PSA levels are not specific to cancer — urinary tract infections and a naturally enlarged prostate (benign prostatic hyperplasia) can also raise PSA concentrations. A positive test result could therefore be a false positive (not due to cancer), leading to unnecessary further investigation or treatment.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.5": "Drugs could reverse epigenetic changes by: (1) increasing methylation of oncogene promoter regions — increased DNA methylation of the promoter silences the oncogene, preventing its transcription and reducing uncontrolled cell division; or (2) decreasing methylation of tumour suppressor gene promoters — restoring tumour suppressor expression allows normal regulation of the cell cycle. Drugs could also alter histone acetylation: increasing histone acetylation of tumour suppressor genes relaxes the chromatin and makes the genes more accessible to RNA polymerase, stimulating transcription and restoring normal cell cycle control.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.1": "Left amino acid: Serine; Middle amino acid: Alanine; Right amino acid: Aspartic acid.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.2": "Row 1 (Contains peptide bonds): Reverse transcriptase ✓ only (it is a protein/enzyme).\nRow 2 (Is formed using a condensation reaction): DNA ✓, ATP ✓, Reverse transcriptase ✓, Phospholipid ✓ — all four.\nRow 3 (Is a polymer): DNA ✓ and Reverse transcriptase ✓ only.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.3": "Beta chain length = 141 × 1.03546 = 145.9994 ≈ 146 amino acids.\nTotal in haemoglobin = (2 × 141) + (2 × 146) = 282 + 292 = 574 amino acids.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.4": "When BPG binds to haemoglobin it reduces affinity for oxygen — the dissociation curve shifts to the right of the normal curve, following the same S-shape but at a lower percentage saturation at any given pO₂. This means haemoglobin loads less oxygen in the lungs and unloads more readily in the tissues.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.5": "It would be an advantage for BPG to bind to haemoglobin during exercise or at high altitude (where tissue pO₂ is low). The reduced affinity means haemoglobin releases more oxygen to the respiring tissues at low pO₂, providing more oxygen for aerobic respiration and delaying the onset of anaerobic respiration.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.1": "P: Capsule/lamellae. Q: Axon (sensory neurone membrane). R: Schwann cell/myelin sheath.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.2": "1.27% (percentage uncertainty = (1 mm ÷ 79 mm) × 100 = 1.27%).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.3": "The value in Trial 3 (10 mm) is anomalously low because the student began to close her fingers before the ruler was released (a pre-emptive/anticipatory movement), so the ruler did not fall as far as it would in a genuine reaction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.4": "Excluding Trial 3, distances are: Trial 1 = 79 mm (128 ms), Trial 2 = 97 mm (≈136 ms), Trial 4 = 94 mm (≈136 ms).\nMean reaction time = (128 + 136 + 136) ÷ 3 = 133 ms = 0.133 s.\nSpeed = distance ÷ time = 1.75 m ÷ 0.133 s ≈ 13.2 m s⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.5": "Three reasons why the estimated speed was less than 76.2 m s⁻¹: (1) time is needed for synaptic transmission at neuromuscular junctions (neurotransmitter release and diffusion across the cleft); (2) time is required for muscle contraction to occur (excitation–contraction coupling) after the action potential arrives; (3) time is needed for the brain to process the sensory information and coordinate the motor response.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.1": "Hydrochloric acid was used to break down the cell walls (cellulose) and the links between cells (middle lamella), separating the cells so they could be squashed into a single layer. It also stops mitosis by denaturing enzymes involved in cell division, fixing the cells in their current stage.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.2": "Pressing the coverslip downwards squashed and spread the root tip cells into a single, thin layer. This allows light to pass through the cells so they can be observed and the chromosomes/stages of mitosis can be clearly seen under the optical microscope (without cells overlapping and obscuring the view).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.3": "Stage of mitosis: Anaphase.\nExplanation: The chromatids have been pulled apart by the spindle fibres to opposite poles/ends of the cell — sister chromatids are visible moving towards opposite ends, so the cell appears elongated with chromosomes at each pole.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.4": "Mitotic index = number of cells in mitosis ÷ total number of cells = 5 ÷ 39 = 0.13.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.5": "Two explanations for different mitotic indices: (1) the garlic roots/plants may have been grown at different temperatures or under different conditions, affecting the rate of cell division; (2) different fields of view were examined from different parts of the root tip — cells in the meristematic zone divide more rapidly than cells further from the tip, so the mitotic index varies depending on which region is observed.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.1": "Genome: the complete set of genes (all the DNA) in a cell or organism.\nProteome: the full range of proteins that a cell or organism can produce, as coded for by the genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.2": "Two reasons bacteria can use human DNA to produce human proteins: (1) the genetic/DNA code is universal — the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms; (2) the mechanism of translation is universal — bacteria have ribosomes (70S) that can translate human mRNA into protein using the same codon–anticodon system.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.3": "Bacteria cannot produce every human protein because they cannot splice pre-mRNA (they have no spliceosomes/spliceosome machinery to remove introns from pre-mRNA). Human genes contain introns that must be removed before the mRNA can be correctly translated — bacteria would translate the intron sequences, producing a non-functional protein.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.4": "Region M: promoter. Region N: terminator.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.5": "The marker gene allows scientists to identify which goat cells or embryos have successfully taken up and integrated the DNA fragment — cells containing the marker gene (e.g. conferring antibiotic resistance or fluorescence) are identified, confirming that the antithrombin gene has also been inserted.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.6": "Two reasons for the importance of the enhancer: (1) antithrombin is only produced in the milk-producing glands, making it easy and harmless to extract (milking); (2) if antithrombin were produced in the bloodstream, it would prevent clotting throughout the body, which could be fatal to the goat.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.1": "Total CO₂ uptake = 8.8 µmol m⁻² s⁻¹ × 12 000 m² × 5700 s = 601 920 000 µmol = 6.0 × 10⁸ µmol.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.2": "The difference is not significant. There is more than a 50% (0.5 = 50%) probability that the observed difference in CO₂ uptake between full sun and shade trees during 08:30–09:40 is due to chance — it is not a real difference attributable to light conditions.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.3": "At very high light intensities, electron release from chlorophyll is inhibited. This means fewer electrons enter the electron transport chain in the thylakoid membrane — less ATP and reduced NADP are produced in the light-dependent reactions. With less reduced NADP and ATP available, the light-independent reaction is slowed: less GP (glycerate-3-phosphate) is reduced to triose phosphate, less RuBP is regenerated, and less CO₂ is fixed. This explains why the rate of CO₂ uptake by full-sun trees at 11:40–13:15 (peak sunlight) is lower than expected — high light inhibits the light-dependent reactions and therefore the overall rate of photosynthesis.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.4": "D = L ÷ (0.4F) = (2.87 × 10⁻²) ÷ (0.4 × 20) = 2.87 × 10⁻² ÷ 8 = 3.59 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻² h⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.5": "Slow-growing, shade-tolerant plants photosynthesise at a low rate (limited by low light intensity), so they grow slowly and take a long time to replace mature leaves if they are eaten. By producing poisonous chemicals in mature leaves, the plants deter herbivores, protecting the leaves from being destroyed — this maintains a sufficient leaf surface area for photosynthesis and the plant's energy budget.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.6": "The data partially support the journalist's summary: the most recently evolved taxa (asterids) do produce above the mean concentration, and ferns (one of the earliest) produce below the mean — consistent with the trend. However, the highest concentration is in magnoliids (not the most recently evolved), and monocots (more recently evolved than ferns) produce less than ferns — this contradicts a simple linear trend. Additionally, basal angiosperms and rosids have the same concentration despite evolving at different times. The data therefore do not fully support the journalist's conclusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::06.1": "A high-band model essay on 'The uses and importance of ATP in organisms' would link at least four specification topics:\n\nATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the universal energy currency of all living organisms. It is a nucleotide consisting of adenosine (ribose + adenine) bonded to three phosphate groups linked by high-energy phosphoanhydride bonds. ATP is synthesised from ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pᵢ) in a condensation reaction catalysed by ATP synthase, and hydrolysed to ADP + Pᵢ by ATPases, releasing ~30 kJ mol⁻¹ of usable energy.\n\nIn respiration, ATP is synthesised by three mechanisms: substrate-level phosphorylation during glycolysis and the Krebs cycle; and oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane, where the electron transport chain creates a proton gradient used by ATP synthase (chemiosmosis). In aerobic respiration, one glucose molecule yields approximately 30–32 ATP. In anaerobic respiration, only 2 ATP are produced per glucose via glycolysis.\n\nATP is essential for active transport: membrane-bound ATPases use ATP hydrolysis to drive ion pumps (e.g. the sodium–potassium pump), maintaining electrochemical gradients critical for nerve impulse transmission and co-transport of glucose and amino acids in the ileum. At neuromuscular junctions, Na⁺/K⁺ ATPases restore resting potential after an action potential, allowing repeated firing.\n\nIn muscle contraction, ATP plays two roles: it binds to the myosin head to break the actinomyosin cross-bridge (allowing myosin to detach from actin); and ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the myosin head for the next power stroke. Without ATP, muscles enter rigor mortis — they remain locked in cross-bridge formation.\n\nIn photosynthesis, ATP is produced in the light-dependent reactions (by photophosphorylation) and consumed in the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) to reduce GP to triose phosphate and regenerate RuBP. ATP links the two stages of photosynthesis.\n\nATP provides energy for biosynthesis: DNA replication (helicase unwinding, polymerase activity), transcription (RNA polymerase), and translation (ribosome activity, tRNA charging) all require ATP. Cell division (mitosis and meiosis) needs ATP to assemble the spindle and move chromosomes. In the immune response, ATP powers the proliferation of B and T cells and the secretion of antibodies by plasma cells.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::06.2": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of cycles in biology' would link at least four specification topics:\n\nCycles are fundamental to biology because they allow the continuous regeneration of essential molecules and the maintenance of steady-state conditions without the need to continually import new raw materials.\n\nThe Calvin cycle (light-independent reaction of photosynthesis) is a biochemical cycle in which CO₂ is fixed by RuBP (ribulose bisphosphate) via Rubisco, producing GP, which is reduced to triose phosphate, and RuBP is regenerated using ATP and reduced NADP. The cycle allows continuous carbon fixation as long as the light-dependent reactions supply ATP and NADPH.\n\nThe Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) is central to aerobic respiration. Acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetate, releasing CO₂ and reducing NAD and FAD in a series of reactions that regenerate oxaloacetate — enabling the cycle to turn again. The reduced coenzymes feed the electron transport chain, driving ATP synthesis by chemiosmosis.\n\nThe cardiac cycle governs the heart's rhythmic contraction and relaxation. Systole (ventricular contraction) pumps blood to the lungs and body; diastole (relaxation and filling) prepares for the next beat. The SAN generates a cyclical wave of depolarisation, transmitted through the AVN and Purkyne fibres. This cycle maintains continuous blood circulation and gas exchange.\n\nThe nitrogen cycle involves the cycling of nitrogen between the atmosphere, soil, and living organisms. Nitrogen fixation (by Rhizobium and Azotobacter) converts N₂ to NH₄⁺; nitrification converts NH₄⁺ to NO₃⁻ (plant-available); plants incorporate NO₃⁻ into amino acids and proteins; decomposers return nitrogen to the soil as NH₄⁺; and denitrification returns N₂ to the atmosphere. This cycle prevents nitrogen depletion and sustains productivity.\n\nThe cell cycle (interphase → mitosis → cytokinesis) allows continuous cell renewal. DNA is replicated during S-phase; chromosomes are separated during mitosis; and two genetically identical daughter cells are produced. This cycle is regulated by cyclins and CDKs; failure of regulation leads to cancer. The meiotic process is also cyclic in sexually reproducing organisms, producing haploid gametes that restore diploid number at fertilisation — cycling genetic variation through generations.\n\nHomeostatic mechanisms involve negative feedback cycles: blood glucose regulation involves a cycle between insulin/glucagon secretion and target-organ response (liver converting glucose to glycogen or vice versa), returning blood glucose to its set point. Similarly, ADH release and water reabsorption form a feedback cycle maintaining blood water potential.",

// ─── Edexcel A (Salters-Nuffield) 9BN0 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────
// Paper 1 — The Natural Environment and Species Survival

"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(a)(i)": "D — 31 874 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹. NPP = GPP − R = 87 069 − 55 195 = 31 874 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(a)(ii)": "Biomass fixed in primary consumers = 4615 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.\nTotal light energy entering = 7 112 800 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.\nPercentage = (4615 ÷ 7 112 800) × 100 = 0.065% (to 2 s.f.).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions play several roles in plant biomass production. Nitrate ions (NO₃⁻) are absorbed by roots and used to synthesise amino acids and proteins (including enzymes and structural proteins) — essential for growth. Magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) are incorporated into chlorophyll molecules; without magnesium, chlorophyll cannot be synthesised and photosynthesis is reduced, limiting biomass production. Phosphate ions (PO₄³⁻) are used to make ATP (for energy supply in photosynthesis and respiration) and nucleotides (for DNA and RNA synthesis), both essential for cell division and growth.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(a)": "In photosynthesis, CO₂ is used in the light-independent (Calvin cycle) reactions in the stroma of the chloroplast. CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP, a 5-carbon compound) in a reaction catalysed by the enzyme Rubisco (RuBisCO), producing two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate (GP, a 3-carbon compound). GP is then reduced using ATP and reduced NADP (both produced in the light-dependent reactions) to form triose phosphate (TP). TP is used to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules, and to regenerate RuBP — allowing CO₂ fixation to continue.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(b)(i)": "C — positive correlation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(b)(ii)": "B — to determine the repeatability of the measurements.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(c)": "Above 1000 ppm CO₂, the rate of photosynthesis may not increase further because other factors become limiting. The enzymes involved in the Calvin cycle (such as Rubisco) have a maximum rate determined by their active site — once all active sites are saturated with substrate, increasing CO₂ concentration cannot increase the reaction rate further. Additionally, light intensity or temperature may be limiting factors — even if more CO₂ is available, insufficient light means the light-dependent reactions cannot produce enough ATP and reduced NADP to drive the Calvin cycle any faster.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(a)": "Digitalis/digoxin (from the foxglove plant, Digitalis purpurea).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(b)": "William Withering carried out: testing of dose (✓). He did NOT use a placebo (✗), did NOT test on healthy volunteers (✗), and did NOT use a double-blind trial (✗).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(c)": "Paclitaxel prevents spindle fibres from shortening/lengthening (depolymerising). During cell division (mitosis), the spindle fibres attach to chromosomes at the centromere and normally shorten to pull the chromatids to opposite poles of the cell during anaphase. If spindle fibres cannot change in length, chromatids cannot be separated and moved to the poles — mitosis is arrested at metaphase/anaphase. As a result, the cancer cells cannot complete cell division and produce daughter cells, so uncontrolled cell division (tumour growth) is prevented.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(a)": "A mutation is a change in the DNA base sequence. A substitution mutation replaces one base with another in the codon, which may change the codon to code for a different amino acid — altering the sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide (the primary structure). An insertion or deletion mutation shifts the reading frame of all subsequent codons (a frameshift), changing many amino acids downstream. A change in the amino acid sequence changes which R groups are present and therefore the interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds, hydrophobic interactions) between them — altering the folding and three-dimensional structure of the protein.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(b)(i)": "The CFTR protein is an integral (transmembrane) protein — it is embedded within and spans the entire phospholipid bilayer of the cell-surface membrane. It passes through the hydrophobic core of the bilayer, with hydrophilic regions exposed on both the cytoplasmic and extracellular sides.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(b)(ii)": "The CFTR protein is synthesised on ribosomes attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum (rER). It is threaded into the rER lumen as it is translated. Vesicles bud off from the rER and fuse with the Golgi apparatus, where the protein is modified (e.g. glycosylated) and packaged into secretory vesicles. These vesicles move to and fuse with the cell-surface membrane (exocytosis), inserting the CFTR protein into the membrane.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(c)": "The primary structure (amino acid sequence) of a protein determines the R groups present at each position. R groups interact with each other through hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds, and hydrophobic/hydrophilic interactions to fold the polypeptide into its specific tertiary structure. A change in the primary structure (from mutation) places a different R group at one position. The pattern of interactions between R groups changes — different bonds form or existing bonds cannot form — causing the polypeptide to fold differently. The resulting tertiary structure (3D shape) of the protein is altered, which in the case of CFTR means the chloride ion channel has the incorrect shape and cannot function properly.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(i)": "D — the thylakoid where the light-dependent reactions take place.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(ii)": "D — the stroma where the light-independent reactions take place.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(iii)": "D — starch.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(b)(i)": "ATP and reduced NADP (NADPH).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(b)(ii)": "Add a known volume of isolated chloroplasts to a solution of DCPIP (blue dye) in a cuvette. DCPIP acts as an electron acceptor — it is reduced by the electrons from the light-dependent reactions and becomes colourless. Set up a series of cuvettes at different temperatures (e.g. 10°C, 20°C, 30°C, 40°C) in a water bath. Illuminate each cuvette with the same light intensity for the same time. Measure the absorbance/transmission of the solution using a colorimeter at set time intervals — a decrease in absorbance (bleaching of the blue colour) indicates reduction of DCPIP and thus the rate of light-dependent reactions. Calculate the rate of colour change at each temperature to determine the effect of temperature.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(a)": "Starch is a polysaccharide made of α-glucose monomers joined by glycosidic bonds formed by condensation reactions. It exists as two forms: amylose — an unbranched, helical chain of glucose molecules linked by α-1,4 glycosidic bonds; and amylopectin — a branched chain with α-1,4 links along the chain and α-1,6 links at branch points. Starch is insoluble in water (making it suitable for storage without affecting water potential) and compact due to its coiled structure.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(b)(i)": "An increase in temperature increases the kinetic energy of enzyme and substrate molecules, increasing the frequency of successful collisions between substrate molecules and the active sites of enzymes (such as amylase). This increases the rate of starch hydrolysis to maltose and then glucose. Glucose is used in respiration to provide energy for germination processes such as cell division and growth. Higher temperature therefore speeds up the metabolic reactions needed for germination.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(b)(ii)": "Climate change could cause higher temperatures in North American woodlands, causing some plant species to shift their ranges northward or to higher altitudes where temperatures remain suitable. Some species adapted to cooler conditions may decline or become locally extinct as temperatures rise above their optimum. New species from warmer regions may colonise areas that were previously too cold. Changes in rainfall patterns could also alter soil moisture and nutrient availability, favouring drought-tolerant species and reducing the ranges of moisture-dependent plants.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(c)": "Cold conditions slow the metabolic rate of the seeds, reducing respiration. This preserves the seed's energy stores (such as starch) and extends viability by slowing the rate at which stored nutrients are consumed. Dry conditions are required because water is needed for enzymatic reactions (including respiration and germination); reducing water content to very low levels prevents germination from beginning prematurely and prevents microbial growth (fungi and bacteria) that would degrade the seeds. Together, cold and dry conditions minimise metabolic activity and prevent deterioration, allowing long-term storage.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(a)(i)": "D — of one species living in the same habitat.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(a)(ii)": "Several deer species can live in the same area because each species occupies a different ecological niche — they exploit different resources and have different habitat preferences. For example, different species may feed at different heights, at different times of day, or on different plant species. This reduces interspecific competition, allowing multiple species to coexist without one excluding the others.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(b)(i)": "White deer = homozygous recessive = q² = 250 ÷ 800 = 0.3125. So q = √0.3125 = 0.559. p = 1 − 0.559 = 0.441. Heterozygotes = 2pq × N = 2 × 0.441 × 0.559 × 800 = 2 × 0.2465 × 800 = 394 heterozygotes (approximately).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(b)(ii)": "C — 1 and 3.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(c)": "Procedure to measure plant biodiversity: (1) Use random number tables to generate coordinates and place quadrats randomly in each area (deer-present and deer-excluded). (2) In each quadrat, record the species present and either count the number of individuals of each species or estimate percentage cover. (3) Repeat with multiple quadrats in each area to obtain a representative sample. (4) Calculate an index of diversity (e.g. Simpson's index: d = 1 − Σn(n−1)/N(N−1)) for each area, where n = number of individuals of each species and N = total individuals. Compare the index of diversity between the two areas — a higher index in the deer-excluded area would indicate that deer grazing reduces plant biodiversity.",

// Paper 2 — Energy, Exercise and Coordination (Edexcel A 2024)
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(i)": "B — the sinoatrial node (SAN) in the right atrium.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(ii)": "The cardiac cycle has two main phases: systole (contraction) and diastole (relaxation). During atrial systole, the atria contract, pushing blood into the ventricles through the atrioventricular valves (mitral and tricuspid), which are open; the semilunar valves are closed. During ventricular systole, the ventricles contract — pressure rises above that in the atria (AV valves close, preventing backflow) and above that in the aorta/pulmonary artery (semilunar valves open), ejecting blood. During diastole, all chambers relax, pressure drops, semilunar valves close (preventing backflow from arteries), and the atria refill with blood returning from the veins.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::2(a)": "Aerobic respiration occurs in three stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm) — glucose is phosphorylated and split into two pyruvate molecules, producing a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 reduced NAD; the link reaction (mitochondrial matrix) — pyruvate is decarboxylated and combined with coenzyme A to form acetyl CoA, producing reduced NAD and CO₂; the Krebs cycle (matrix) — acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetate, releasing CO₂ and producing reduced NAD, reduced FAD, and 1 ATP per turn. The reduced coenzymes (NADH, FADH₂) donate electrons to the electron transport chain (inner mitochondrial membrane), where their energy drives proton pumping across the membrane, creating a gradient used by ATP synthase (chemiosmosis) to produce the majority of ATP. Oxygen acts as the terminal electron acceptor, combining with protons and electrons to form water.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(a)": "The nervous system and the endocrine (hormonal) system are the two main communication systems. The nervous system transmits electrical impulses along neurones — the signal is fast, short-lived, and localised to specific target cells (effectors) connected by synapses. Acetylcholine or noradrenaline are released at synapses. The endocrine system uses hormones secreted by glands directly into the bloodstream — signals are slower, longer-lasting, and act on widespread target cells with specific receptors. Both systems work together in homeostasis: for example, a decrease in blood glucose is detected by the pancreas (endocrine: glucagon released to stimulate glycogenolysis) and can also trigger adrenaline release (adrenal gland), which prepares the body for fight-or-flight via both nervous and hormonal pathways.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(a)": "Skeletal muscle fibre structure: each fibre is a large, multinucleate cell surrounded by the sarcolemma (cell-surface membrane). The sarcoplasm contains many myofibrils, made up of repeating units called sarcomeres. Each sarcomere contains thick myosin filaments and thin actin filaments. In a resting sarcomere, actin filaments extend inward from the Z lines, overlapping partially with myosin. The I band (actin only) and H zone (myosin only) are visible between the A band (full myosin length, including overlap). During contraction, calcium ions released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin to expose myosin-binding sites on actin. Myosin heads bind actin (forming cross-bridges), perform a power stroke (using ATP hydrolysis), and the actin filaments slide inward — the sarcomere shortens, reducing the I band and H zone but maintaining the A band length.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(a)": "Homeostasis maintains a constant internal environment. Body temperature is regulated by negative feedback: thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus and skin detect deviations from the set point (37°C). If body temperature rises, the hypothalamus sends signals to promote vasodilation (increasing blood flow to the skin), sweating (evaporative cooling), and reduced metabolic heat production — returning temperature to normal. If temperature falls, vasoconstriction, shivering (generating heat from muscle contraction), and increased metabolic rate restore it. The set point acts as a reference; when the effectors' response brings the variable back to the set point, the stimulus is removed and the corrective response stops.",

// Paper 3 — General and Practical Applications (Edexcel A 2024)
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(a)": "PCR (polymerase chain reaction) amplifies a specific DNA sequence. The DNA is heated to ~95°C to denature the double helix (separate strands). It is cooled to ~55°C so that primers (short complementary oligonucleotide sequences) anneal to each strand flanking the target region. The mixture is heated to ~72°C — the optimum for Taq DNA polymerase, which extends the new strand from each primer in the 5'→3' direction using free nucleotides. After many cycles (typically 30), millions of copies of the target sequence are produced.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::2(a)": "Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size. DNA is negatively charged (phosphate backbone), so it migrates towards the positive electrode in an electric field. Smaller fragments move faster and further through the agarose gel matrix; larger fragments move more slowly and remain closer to the wells. After electrophoresis, the gel is stained (e.g. with ethidium bromide or SYBR Green) and visualised under UV light — each fragment appears as a band. Comparing band positions with a DNA ladder (known size markers) allows fragment sizes to be determined.",

// ─── Edexcel A 9BN0 — June 2023 ──────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(a)": "NPP = GPP − R. Net primary productivity is the energy available to consumers after subtracting plant respiration from gross primary productivity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions are essential for plant biomass production. Nitrate ions provide nitrogen for synthesising amino acids, proteins, and nucleic acids — all key components of cell growth. Phosphate ions are needed for ATP synthesis (energy for metabolic processes) and for DNA/RNA (nucleotides). Magnesium ions are required for chlorophyll synthesis — without adequate magnesium, the plant cannot photosynthesize efficiently and biomass production is reduced.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(a)": "CO₂ diffuses into the leaf through stomata and into the stroma of the chloroplast. In the Calvin cycle, CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) via the enzyme Rubisco to form two molecules of 3-carbon glycerate-3-phosphate (GP). GP is reduced to triose phosphate using ATP and reduced NADP from the light-dependent reactions. Triose phosphate is used to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules, or to regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::3(a)": "Clinical trials involve phases: Phase 1 tests safety in healthy volunteers at low doses; Phase 2 tests efficacy and dose in small groups of patients; Phase 3 is a large randomised controlled trial comparing the new drug against a placebo or existing treatment, usually double-blind. Double-blind means neither the patient nor the clinician knows who receives the drug or placebo, eliminating bias. A placebo controls for the psychological effect of receiving treatment. Randomisation of participants into drug and placebo groups reduces confounding variables.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::4(a)": "A gene mutation changes the DNA base sequence. A point substitution replaces one base, potentially changing the codon to code for a different amino acid (missense mutation), altering the primary structure. An insertion or deletion causes a frameshift, changing the reading frame of all subsequent codons and radically altering the amino acid sequence downstream. Changes in primary structure affect R group interactions, altering folding and the 3D tertiary structure of the protein.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(a)": "The light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes. Light energy is absorbed by photosystems (PS I and PS II), exciting electrons to higher energy levels. In PS II, water molecules are split (photolysis) to replace lost electrons, releasing O₂ and H⁺ ions. Excited electrons pass along the electron transport chain, releasing energy used to pump H⁺ into the thylakoid lumen, creating a proton gradient. H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase (chemiosmosis), generating ATP. At PS I, electrons are re-energised and used (with H⁺) to reduce NADP to NADPH.",

// ─── Edexcel A 9BN0 — June 2022 ──────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(a)": "NPP = GPP − R. The net primary productivity is the chemical energy stored as plant biomass per unit area per unit time, available for consumption by primary consumers.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions are absorbed from the soil via root hair cells by active transport. Nitrate ions (NO₃⁻) provide nitrogen for amino acid and protein synthesis. Phosphate ions (PO₄³⁻) are needed for ATP and nucleotide production. Magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) are incorporated into the porphyrin ring of chlorophyll. These ions are essential for producing the enzymes, nucleic acids, and pigments that drive growth and photosynthesis, directly contributing to plant biomass.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::1(a)": "The heart's electrical activity follows the cardiac cycle. The sinoatrial node (SAN) in the right atrial wall generates spontaneous action potentials that spread across both atria causing atrial systole. The wave of depolarisation reaches the atrioventricular node (AVN), which introduces a brief delay (allowing ventricles to fill). It then passes down the bundle of His and Purkyne fibres to the apex of the ventricles, causing ventricular systole from the bottom upward, pumping blood into the aorta and pulmonary artery.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::1(a)": "A valid investigation requires: a control (untreated cells to compare against); multiple replicates to assess variability; controlled variables (same temperature, pH, incubation time); random sampling to avoid bias; use of a colorimeter or other objective measurement rather than visual judgement.",

// ─── Edexcel B (Advancing Biology) 9BI0 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────
// Paper 1 — Advanced Biochemistry, Microbiology and Genetics
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::1(a)": "DNA is a double-stranded polynucleotide. Each nucleotide consists of a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group, and one of four nitrogenous bases (adenine, thymine, cytosine, or guanine). Adjacent nucleotides within a strand are joined by phosphodiester bonds (between the 3' carbon of one deoxyribose and the 5' carbon of the next via the phosphate group). The two antiparallel strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: adenine–thymine (two hydrogen bonds) and cytosine–guanine (three hydrogen bonds). The double helix is stabilised by base stacking interactions between adjacent base pairs.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::2(a)": "Gene expression involves transcription and translation. In transcription, RNA polymerase binds to the promoter region of a gene on the template strand and unwinds the DNA. Free RNA nucleotides base-pair with the template strand (A–U, T–A, G–C), and RNA polymerase joins them by phosphodiester bonds, forming a pre-mRNA strand. In eukaryotes, introns are removed by splicing (by spliceosomes) and exons are joined to form mature mRNA. The mRNA leaves the nucleus through nuclear pores. In translation, mRNA binds to ribosomes; tRNA molecules with complementary anticodons carry specific amino acids and bind to codons on the mRNA; peptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids as the ribosome moves along the mRNA, elongating the polypeptide chain.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::3(a)": "Enzymes are biological catalysts that lower the activation energy of reactions by forming enzyme–substrate complexes. The active site has a specific 3D shape complementary to the substrate. In the induced fit model, the active site changes shape slightly as the substrate binds, improving complementarity and straining bonds in the substrate, facilitating the reaction. Enzyme activity is affected by temperature (increases rate up to the optimum, then denatures the enzyme above it) and pH (alters ionisation of R groups in the active site; extreme pH denatures enzymes).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p2::1(a)": "Osmoregulation in mammals involves the kidneys and ADH. Blood water potential is monitored by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus. When blood osmolarity rises (water potential falls), the posterior pituitary releases more ADH. ADH binds to V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, triggering insertion of aquaporins into the luminal membrane. Water moves by osmosis from the collecting duct filtrate into the hypertonic medullary interstitium and then the blood, producing a small volume of concentrated urine. When blood osmolarity falls, less ADH is released, fewer aquaporins are inserted, less water is reabsorbed, and more dilute urine is produced. This negative feedback maintains blood water potential near the set point.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p3::1(a)": "Planning an investigation: identify the independent variable (e.g. enzyme concentration), dependent variable (rate of product formation), and controlled variables (temperature, pH, substrate concentration, buffer). Use replicates (≥3) to assess variability. Include a negative control (no enzyme or boiled enzyme). Measure at regular intervals; use a colorimeter for objective readings. Plot results with error bars and use an appropriate statistical test (e.g. t-test or ANOVA) to determine significance.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::1(a)": "The cell cycle consists of interphase (G1 — cell growth and protein synthesis; S — DNA replication; G2 — organelle replication and further growth) followed by mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) and cytokinesis. During S phase, each chromosome is replicated semiconservatively: the double helix unwinds (helicase), each strand acts as a template, and DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides in the 5'→3' direction, producing two identical sister chromatids joined at the centromere.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p2::1(a)": "The nervous system transmits information as electrical impulses. A resting neurone has a resting potential of approximately −70 mV (inside negative), maintained by the sodium–potassium pump (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP). When stimulated above threshold, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open — Na⁺ rushes in (depolarisation) — reversing the membrane potential to approximately +40 mV. Voltage-gated K⁺ channels then open — K⁺ leaves — repolarising and briefly hyperpolarising the membrane (refractory period). The action potential propagates along the axon (in myelinated neurones, by saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p3::1(a)": "Gel electrophoresis of DNA: pour agarose gel into a tray with wells; allow to set. Load DNA samples mixed with loading buffer (contains dye and glycerol) into wells. Load a DNA ladder in adjacent well. Run electrophoresis at constant voltage in TAE buffer. DNA migrates toward positive electrode — smaller fragments travel further. Stain with ethidium bromide or SYBR Green; visualise under UV. Compare band positions to ladder to determine fragment sizes.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p1::1(a)": "Meiosis produces genetically diverse haploid gametes. Sources of variation: (1) independent assortment — during meiosis I, homologous chromosome pairs align randomly at the metaphase plate; each cell receives a random mix of maternal and paternal chromosomes, producing 2²³ possible combinations in humans; (2) crossing over — during prophase I, non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes exchange segments at chiasmata, creating new combinations of alleles on each chromosome (recombinant chromosomes); (3) random fertilisation — any sperm can fertilise any egg, creating further variation.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p2::1(a)": "The immune response has two arms. The innate immune response is non-specific and immediate: phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) engulf pathogens by phagocytosis — lysosomes fuse with phagosomes, hydrolyzing the pathogen. Macrophages act as antigen-presenting cells (APCs), displaying antigens on MHC class II molecules. The adaptive immune response is specific and slower: T helper cells (with complementary T cell receptors) bind to APC-presented antigens and become activated; they release cytokines stimulating B cell activation and T cytotoxic cell proliferation. B cells differentiate into plasma cells (producing specific antibodies) and memory B cells. Antibodies neutralise pathogens, agglutinate them, or opsonise them for phagocytosis.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p3::1(a)": "Evaluating experimental data: consider sample size (larger = more reliable); whether a control was included; identify potential sources of error (systematic error, random error); consider whether results are repeatable and reproducible. Apply an appropriate statistical test to determine whether differences are significant (P < 0.05 means less than 5% probability the difference is due to chance). Consider whether the method measures what it claims to (validity) and whether results can be generalised (external validity).",

// ─── OCR A Biology H420 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ────────────────────────────────

// Paper 1 — Biological processes
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::1(a)": "The phospholipid bilayer is the basic structure of the cell-surface membrane. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic phosphate head (facing the aqueous environments inside and outside the cell) and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails (facing inward, away from water). This arrangement creates a selectively permeable barrier. Cholesterol molecules interspersed between phospholipids reduce fluidity and permeability. Proteins embedded in the bilayer act as channels, carriers, receptors, and enzymes. Glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer surface act as cell-surface receptors and recognition sites (e.g. for cell signalling, the immune system, and cell adhesion).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::2(a)": "Enzymes lower activation energy by forming enzyme-substrate complexes at the active site. The complementary shape of the active site to the substrate (lock and key) or the conformational change upon substrate binding (induced fit) positions reactive groups precisely, straining bonds and facilitating the reaction. Competitive inhibitors have a similar shape to the substrate and compete for the active site — increasing substrate concentration can overcome inhibition. Non-competitive inhibitors bind to an allosteric site, changing the shape of the active site so the substrate can no longer bind — inhibition cannot be overcome by increasing substrate concentration.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::3(a)": "Gas exchange in mammals: O₂ diffuses from alveolar air into alveolar epithelial cells, then into blood plasma and red blood cells where it binds to haemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin. CO₂ diffuses from red blood cells (where it is produced in the Krebs cycle) into the alveoli. The large surface area of alveoli, thin walls (one cell thick), moist surface, and rich capillary network maintain steep concentration gradients for efficient gas exchange. Ventilation constantly replenishes the air in the alveoli (tidal volume + dead space), maintaining the gradient.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p2::1(a)": "Natural selection: individuals in a population show variation (due to mutations and sexual reproduction). In a given environment, individuals with alleles that improve survival and reproduction are more likely to survive long enough to reproduce and pass on those alleles (differential reproductive success). Over generations, the frequency of advantageous alleles increases in the population. Speciation occurs when populations become reproductively isolated — allele frequencies diverge sufficiently that they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p3::1(a)": "Experimental design for an enzyme investigation: independent variable — substrate concentration (or pH, temperature). Dependent variable — rate of reaction (e.g. time for colour change, or gas volume collected). Control variables — enzyme concentration, temperature, pH, volume of reactants. Controls — a blank (no enzyme, or boiled enzyme) to account for spontaneous reaction. Replicates — at least three per condition. Statistical analysis — use a t-test or Mann-Whitney U test to compare means; state the null hypothesis (no significant difference between conditions).",

// Paper 2 — Biological diversity
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p1::1(a)": "Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells from one parent cell. During prophase, chromosomes condense and the nuclear envelope breaks down; during metaphase, chromosomes align at the metaphase plate; during anaphase, centromeres split and sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles by spindle fibres; during telophase, nuclear envelopes reform around each set of chromosomes. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm. Mitosis is used for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p2::1(a)": "Biodiversity can be measured at three levels: genetic diversity (variation in allele frequencies within a species), species diversity (number and relative abundance of species in an area — measured by indices such as Simpson's), and ecosystem diversity (variety of habitats and communities). Biodiversity is maintained by protecting habitats (nature reserves, marine protected areas), sustainable management, captive breeding and reintroduction programmes, seed banks, and international conservation agreements (e.g. the Convention on Biological Diversity). Human activities that threaten biodiversity include habitat destruction, pollution, invasive species, overexploitation, and climate change.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p3::1(a)": "In a synoptic question linking topics: homeostasis maintains internal conditions within narrow limits via negative feedback. The hypothalamus monitors blood temperature and osmolarity; effectors (sweat glands, muscles, blood vessels) correct deviations. The endocrine system (hormones) coordinates longer-term responses while the nervous system provides rapid, localised responses. Both systems converge on target cells with specific receptors.",

// Paper 3 — Unified biology
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p1::1(a)": "In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus. RNA polymerase binds to the promoter, unwinds the DNA, and synthesises a pre-mRNA strand complementary to the template strand. The pre-mRNA undergoes processing: a 5' cap and poly-A tail are added (protecting the mRNA from degradation and facilitating ribosome binding), and introns are removed by spliceosomes (leaving exons joined to form mature mRNA). The mature mRNA is exported to the cytoplasm for translation at ribosomes.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p2::1(a)": "Ecological sampling techniques: transects (line or belt) used to study changes in species distribution across an environmental gradient (e.g. from sea to land); quadrats used to estimate abundance and frequency within an area — placed randomly using random number tables; mark-release-recapture for mobile animals. Data collected include species present, percentage cover or ACFOR scale, and numbers. Abiotic factors (light intensity, pH, soil moisture) are measured alongside to explain distribution patterns.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p3::1(a)": "Photosynthesis and respiration are linked through ATP and reduced coenzymes. In the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, light energy is used to produce ATP and NADPH; these drive the Calvin cycle (light-independent) to fix CO₂ into triose phosphate and glucose. In aerobic respiration, glucose is oxidised through glycolysis, the link reaction, the Krebs cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP, CO₂, and water. The CO₂ from respiration can be fixed again in photosynthesis, linking the two processes in the carbon cycle.",

// ─── OCR B (Advancing Biology) H422 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────

"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p1::1(a)": "Cells are the basic structural and functional unit of life. All cells are surrounded by a cell-surface membrane (phospholipid bilayer), contain DNA (as the genetic material), and use ribosomes for protein synthesis. Eukaryotic cells (animals, plants, fungi) have a membrane-bound nucleus containing linear chromosomes, and membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes). Prokaryotic cells (bacteria) have no nucleus — DNA is circular and free in the cytoplasm; they have 70S ribosomes, a cell wall of peptidoglycan, and may have plasmids, a capsule, and flagella.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p2::1(a)": "Evolution by natural selection: within a population there is genetic variation (due to mutation and sexual reproduction). In a particular environment, individuals with alleles that improve survival and/or reproduction have greater reproductive fitness. They are more likely to survive, reproduce, and pass their alleles to offspring. Over generations, the frequency of advantageous alleles increases. Accumulated genetic changes can lead to speciation if populations become reproductively isolated.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p3::1(a)": "Genetic engineering (recombinant DNA technology): a gene of interest is isolated using restriction endonucleases that cut at specific palindromic sequences, producing complementary sticky ends. The same enzyme cuts a vector (e.g. a bacterial plasmid). The gene is inserted into the vector by complementary base pairing of sticky ends and sealed by DNA ligase. The recombinant plasmid is introduced into a host cell (e.g. bacteria by transformation); cells that have taken up the vector are identified using selectable markers (antibiotic resistance or reporter genes). The host cells express the human gene, producing the protein of interest (e.g. insulin).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p1::1(a)": "Protein synthesis requires transcription and translation. The gene's template strand is transcribed by RNA polymerase (nucleotides added in 5'→3' direction, complementary to the template). The mRNA carries codons (three-base sequences); each codon is recognised by a tRNA anticodon carrying a specific amino acid. Peptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids as the ribosome moves along the mRNA from the start codon (AUG) to the stop codon.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p2::1(a)": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts. The light-dependent reactions on thylakoid membranes produce ATP and NADPH (from photolysis of water and the electron transport chain). These products drive the Calvin cycle in the stroma: CO₂ is fixed by Rubisco onto RuBP, producing GP; GP is reduced (using ATP and NADPH) to triose phosphate; TP is used to make glucose and to regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p3::1(a)": "Respiration produces ATP for cellular work. Glycolysis (cytoplasm): glucose → 2 pyruvate + 2 ATP + 2 NADH. Link reaction (mitochondrial matrix): pyruvate → acetyl CoA + CO₂ + NADH. Krebs cycle (matrix): acetyl CoA → CO₂ + NADH + FADH₂ + 1 ATP. Oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane): NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to the ETC; protons pumped into intermembrane space create a gradient; ATP synthase uses the gradient (chemiosmosis) to produce ATP; O₂ is the final electron acceptor, forming water.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p1::1(a)": "The cell cycle is divided into interphase (G1, S, G2) and the mitotic phase (M phase). G1: cell grows, proteins synthesised. S: DNA replicates semiconservatively. G2: organelles replicate, cell checks for DNA damage. M phase: mitosis (PMAT) followed by cytokinesis. Checkpoints (G1/S, G2/M, spindle checkpoint) ensure the cycle only proceeds if conditions are met. Cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs) regulate progression through checkpoints. Uncontrolled passage through checkpoints leads to cancer.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p2::1(a)": "Variation within a population is essential for natural selection. Genetic variation arises from mutations (random changes in DNA base sequence), independent assortment and crossing over during meiosis, and random fertilisation. Environmental factors can also cause phenotypic variation (not heritable). Selection acts on phenotype — individuals whose phenotype is best suited to the current environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on their alleles. Directional selection shifts the mean phenotype; stabilising selection reduces variation around the mean; disruptive selection favours extreme phenotypes.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p3::1(a)": "Ecology investigates relationships between organisms and their environment. A habitat is the physical location where an organism lives; a niche includes all biotic and abiotic interactions. Food chains and food webs describe energy transfer between trophic levels (producer → primary consumer → secondary consumer). Energy is lost at each level (respiration, excretion, faeces), so typically only 10% transfers between trophic levels. Ecosystems are maintained by nutrient cycling (nitrogen cycle, carbon cycle) and energy flow from the sun.",

};
